      \field{abstract}{Automatically generating unit tests is a powerful approach to exercise complex software. Unfortunately, current techniques often fail to provide relevant input values, such as strings that bypass domain-speciﬁc sanity checks. As a result, state-of-the-art techniques are effective for generic classes, such as collections, but less successful for domain-speciﬁc software. This paper presents TestMiner, the ﬁrst technique for mining a corpus of existing tests for input values to be used by test generators for effectively testing software not in the corpus. The main idea is to extract literals from thousands of tests and to adapt information retrieval techniques to ﬁnd values suitable for a particular domain. Evaluating the approach with 40 Java classes from 18 different projects shows that TestMiner improves test coverage by 21\% over an existing test generator. The approach can be integrated into various test generators in a straightforward way, increasing their effectiveness on previously difﬁcult-to-test classes.}
